<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Sievershausen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1553">1553</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-margrave-war">Second Margrave War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Sievershausen was fought on 9 July 1553 near Lehrte in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> between Elector Maurice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Margrave Albert <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcibiades">Alcibiades</a> of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a>, whose plundering campaigns threatened the peace of the Empire. Maurice won, but he was mortally wounded and died two days later. It was one of the bloodiest battles fought in sixteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sievershausen.jpg" alt="Battle of Sievershausen" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sievershausen_Schlacht.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Margrave Albert Alcibiades of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a> had started the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-margrave-war">Second Margrave War</a> against the Franconian prince-bishoprics in 1552, and his plundering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> army cut a path of destruction on its way to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In the Duchy of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> he campaigned against Duke Henry V of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, who won the support of Elector Maurice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and of his own Lüneburg cousins. Maurice had just signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-passau">Peace of Passau</a> with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> as a leader of the insurgent Protestant princes, and the disorder caused by his former ally came at a bad time for him.</p><p>The two sides first met on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a> river near Sarstedt, where no action was taken. Albert then headed for the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, but the forces of Maurice and Henry V blocked his passage on the Fuhse creek near the village of Sievershausen, which is today part of Lehrte.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The battle was fought in open fields between Sievershausen and Arpke. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> troops attacked with about 15,500 men, 8,000 infantry and 7,500 cavalry, and 25 guns; Albert commanded 18,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, 12,000 of them infantry and 6,000 cavalry. The cavalry of both sides fired their pistols at close range, causing heavy losses, before charging. Albert at first beat back the attack, but the Saxon army turned and took up position behind his troops. After four hours of fighting the margrave fled, his retreat covered by General Joachim von Röbel, who ended his days as a field marshal.</p><p>About 4,000 men were killed, among them many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> nobles and knights. Henry V lost his two elder sons, Philip Magnus and Charles Victor, so that the succession passed to their younger brother Julius. Maurice is said to have been struck in the abdomen by a shot from one of his own men, and he most likely died of an infected wound. His body was taken back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and his armour, kept in Freiberg Cathedral, shows the bullet hole at the waist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Albert's defeat secured the public peace, which was made lasting in the Augsburg Settlement two years later. On the 300th anniversary of the battle in 1853 a memorial stone was set up to mark Maurice's death. Since 1967 the parish church of Sievershausen has been a meeting place for groups of the German peace movement, and an anti-war museum opened in the village in 1979.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Sievershausen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maurice of Saxony, who had sided with the emperor and received the electorate, turned against him in the Princes’ Revolt of 1552, forcing the Peace of Passau, and died in 1553 from wounds received at the <b>Battle of Sievershausen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li></ul></section>
